WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.240
Permissions describe allowed actions on resources.

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:08.260
A permission might allow reading an object, starting a virtual machine, listing secrets,

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:12.560
changing a firewall rule, creating a key, or updating a policy.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:16.860
Policies are the rules that grant, deny, or condition those permissions.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:23.400
They may be attached to an identity, a resource, a group, a role, an organization,

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:28.580
a folder, a subscription, a project, or another scope depending on the provider.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.520
Cloud environments use several access models.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:34.220
Role-based access control, or RBAC,

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:37.560
grants roles that bundle permissions for a job or scope.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:42.900
Attribute-based access control, or ABAC, uses attributes such as tags, labels,

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:46.640
departments, environments, resource names,

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:49.580
or identity properties to make access decisions.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.760
Identity-based policies travel with the principal.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:56.260
Resource-based policies define who can access a resource.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:00.240
Conditions can limit when, where, or how access is used.

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:02.940
Explicit deny rules can override grants.

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:08.040
Effective access is the access a principal actually has after all relevant rules are

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:09.300
evaluated.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:12.800
It can be different from what one visible role assignment suggests.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:15.260
A user may inherit access from a group,

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:20.600
receive additional access through a role assumption path, be blocked by an explicit deny,

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:23.540
or gain access through a resource policy.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:28.560
A workload may look limited in one project but have a token that can impersonate a service

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:29.900
account elsewhere.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:33.480
This is why IAM review must look beyond the first role name.

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:36.900
Practical IAM analysis asks several questions.

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:39.120
What principal is requesting access?

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:40.940
What action is requested?

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:42.760
Which resource is affected?

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:47.460
What policies apply from identity, resource, and inherited scopes?

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:49.040
Are there conditions?

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:50.780
Is there an explicit deny?

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.560
Is access temporary or standing?

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:56.900
Does another path allow impersonation or delegation?

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.920
Understanding effective access is essential for least privilege, troubleshooting,

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:04.540
incident response, and governance.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:07.320
Policy evaluation can also cross boundaries.

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:13.380
A project-level grant, subscription-level role, organization policy, bucket policy,

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:17.600
key policy, or service control may all affect the final answer.

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:22.140
Troubleshooting access often means tracing the request through several layers.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.200
Governance review works the same way.

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:30.420
A visible assignment may look safe until inherited permissions or a resource policy adds a

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:31.760
wider path.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:33.900
The practical outcome is humility.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:37.720
Do not assume a principal has only the permissions you remember assigning.

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:44.420
Use provider tools, access analysis, logs, and policy review to understand actual access.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:50.000
Effective access is what matters during an incident, an audit, or a design review.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:54.620
It is also what matters when you decide whether a role is truly least privilege.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:57.640
Access design should also account for failure modes.

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:00.900
If a condition depends on a tag, who can change that tag?

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:04.400
If access depends on group membership, who manages the group?

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:08.140
If a resource policy grants access to another account or tenant,

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:10.120
who reviews that relationship?

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:14.900
Effective access includes both the grant and the governance around the grant.
